The trp attenuator region encodes a leader transcript with four segments (1–4) that can base-pair in alternative combinations. The 3:4 stem-loop (followed by a run of U residues) is the functional rho-independent terminator. Under tryptophan-rich conditions the ribosome does not stall in segment 1, proceeds through segment 2, and thereby prevents formation of the 2:3 antiterminator; segment 3 is therefore free to pair with segment 4 and transcription aborts.

A mutation that blocks pairing of segment 1 with segment 2 leaves segment 2 unpaired. Segment 2 can consequently base-pair with segment 3 as soon as segment 3 is transcribed, sequestering segment 3 and preventing the 3:4 terminator from forming. Consequently the polymerase continues into the structural genes even when tryptophan is abundant.

Option B blocks the very interaction (2:3) needed for antitermination and therefore favors termination. Option C alters only the downstream U-run, not the 3:4 stem-loop itself. Options D and E affect expression level but do not alter the secondary-structure decision that controls attenuation. Thus only A directly precludes 3:4 formation under repressing conditions.}

