2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The canonical attenuation logic remains the strongest guide here. Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome covers region 2, freeing region 3 to pair with region 4 and form the terminator. A mutation that blocks region 1 from pairing with region 2 removes the early 1–2 competition, so region 2 is available to base-pair with the newly transcribed region 3; the resulting 2–3 antiterminator sequesters region 3 and directly prevents 3–4 formation, allowing read-through.  

Colleague 15’s suggestion (C) only alters the downstream U-run after an intact 3–4 hairpin has already formed; that change affects release efficiency but does not block hairpin assembly itself and therefore cannot satisfy the stated phenotype of preventing 3–4 formation. Options B, D and E either reinforce the terminator or affect initiation/translation rates without altering hairpin choice. Only A directly disrupts the structural decision that normally produces the 3–4 terminator under repressing conditions.}

