The trp leader transcript forms mutually exclusive structures (1-2 pause, 2-3 antiterminator, 3-4 terminator) whose choice is dictated by ribosome position on the leader peptide. Under high tryptophan the ribosome translates without stalling, covers segment 2, and thereby frees segment 3 to pair with segment 4. A mutation that prevents segment 1 from pairing with segment 2 leaves segment 2 free to sequester the newly transcribed segment 3, locking in the 2-3 antiterminator and directly blocking assembly of the 3-4 stem-loop itself. This produces read-through even when tryptophan is abundant.

Option C changes only the downstream U-run; the 3-4 hairpin still forms normally, so the mutation does not satisfy the question’s explicit requirement that formation of the stem-loop be prevented. B would favor rather than block 3-4 pairing, while D and E leave the secondary-structure decision untouched. Thus only A produces the stated phenotype.}

