8.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A

The group’s convergence on A remains mechanistically consistent with the canonical model of mutually exclusive, kinetically ordered hairpins. Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome normally masks region 2, allowing 3–4 terminator formation. When 1–2 pairing is eliminated, region 2 stays single-stranded through the narrow ~50-nt window after region 3 is transcribed; this permits immediate nucleation of the thermodynamically favored 2–3 antiterminator, sequestering the sequences required for the 3–4 stem-loop before region 4 can compete. The result is read-through regardless of ribosome position—precisely the phenotype demanded by the question.

Option C, by contrast, leaves the 3–4 hairpin nucleotides and their co-transcriptional folding pathway unchanged; it only weakens the downstream U-run that normally facilitates release after an already-formed terminator. Because the structural decision itself is unaltered, C cannot satisfy the explicit requirement that formation of the 3–4 stem-loop be prevented. All other choices either stabilize the terminator or act outside hairpin selection. The kinetic-preemption logic therefore continues to identify A as the sole mutation that blocks terminator assembly under repressing conditions.}

